statement:
In a non-equilateral triangle $ABC$ the sides $a, b, c$ form an arithmetic progression. Let $I$ be the incentre and $O$ the circumcentre of triangle $ABC$. Prove that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(1)] $IO$ is perpendicular to $BI$;
\item[(2)] If $BI$ meets $AC$ in $K$, and $D$, $E$ are the midpoints of $BC$, $BA$ respectively, then $I$ is the circumcentre of triangle $DKE$.
\end{enumerate}

In a cyclic quadrilateral $ABCD$, $AB = a$, $BC = b$, $CD = c$, $\angle ABC = 120^\circ$ and $\angle ABD = 30^\circ$. Prove that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(1)] $c \geq a + b$;
\item[(2)] $\left|\sqrt{c + a} - \sqrt{c + b}\right| = \sqrt{c - a - b}$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that if $n$ is an integer such that $n \geq 4011^2$, then there exists an integer $l$ such that $n < l^2 < \left(1 + \dfrac{1}{2005}\right)n$.
\item[(b)] Find the smallest positive integer $M$ for which whenever an integer $n$ satisfies $n \geq M$, there exists an integer $l$ such that $n < l^2 < \left(1 + \dfrac{1}{2005}\right)n$.
\end{enumerate}
